	<Fragment slot="intro">
		<p>
			Whether your GFR can go up depends on why it is low. After a temporary problem such as acute
			kidney injury, eGFR often recovers once the cause is treated. With chronic kidney disease, the
			damage is usually permanent, so the realistic goal is to slow further decline. Slowing it can
			still make a large difference to how long your kidneys keep working.
		</p>
	</Fragment>

	<h2>When eGFR can go back up</h2>
	<ul>
		<li><strong>Acute kidney injury.</strong> A sudden drop usually improves when the cause is treated, such as severe dehydration, infection, or a urinary blockage.</li>
		<li><strong>Dehydration.</strong> Rehydrating can correct a reading that was low for this reason.</li>
		<li><strong>A temporary creatinine rise.</strong> A big meat meal, creatine supplements, or intense exercise before the test can lower the estimate without long-term damage. A repeat test under normal conditions may read higher.</li>
		<li><strong>Normal variation.</strong> eGFR shifts a little from test to test, so the trend matters more than any one result.</li>
	</ul>

	<h2>How to protect your kidneys with CKD</h2>
	<p>The National Kidney Foundation recommends these steps to slow kidney damage:</p>
	<ol>
		<li><strong>Control blood pressure and blood sugar.</strong> Take medicines as prescribed and aim for the targets your clinician sets.</li>
		<li><strong>Eat a kidney-friendly diet.</strong> Keep sodium under 2,300 mg a day, limit saturated fat and added sugar, and eat more fruit, vegetables, legumes, and whole grains.</li>
		<li><strong>Limit NSAIDs.</strong> Ibuprofen and naproxen can harm the kidneys at high doses or with frequent use. Ask about other options for pain.</li>
		<li><strong>Stay active.</strong> Regular exercise helps blood pressure, blood sugar, and heart health.</li>
		<li><strong>Stop smoking.</strong> Smoking damages blood vessels, including those in the kidneys.</li>
		<li><strong>Ask about kidney-protective medicines.</strong> These include ACE inhibitors or ARBs, SGLT2 inhibitors, and nonsteroidal MRAs. Some can cause a small early dip in eGFR, so do not stop one on your own.</li>
	</ol>

	<h2>Will drinking more water raise your GFR?</h2>
	<p>
		If you are dehydrated, yes, drinking enough can bring a low reading back up. Beyond that, extra
		water has not been shown to help. In the CKD WIT randomized trial, coaching adults with CKD to
		drink more water did not slow their decline in kidney function over one year. Too much fluid can
		also be harmful with advanced kidney disease or heart problems, so ask your doctor how much is
		right for you.
	</p>

	<h2>Does any food increase GFR?</h2>
	<p>
		No single food has been shown to raise GFR, and the National Kidney Foundation advises caution
		with any diet that claims to reverse kidney disease. Cutting back on red meat can lower creatinine,
		which may raise the eGFR number without necessarily changing how well the kidneys filter. A
		kidney dietitian can tailor advice to your stage, since needs for protein, potassium, and phosphorus
		change as CKD progresses.
	</p>

	<h2>What to avoid</h2>
	<ul>
		<li><strong>Supplements and herbal products.</strong> Check with your clinician first. Some can harm the kidneys, and "detox" or "kidney cleanse" products have no proven benefit.</li>
		<li><strong>Stopping prescribed medicines.</strong> Blood pressure and diabetes medicines are often what protects your kidneys.</li>
		<li><strong>Judging progress on one test.</strong> Look at several results over time, using the same equation.</li>
	</ul>

	<h2>Can your result look better without your kidneys changing?</h2>
	<p>
		Yes. Standard eGFR is calculated from creatinine, which comes from muscle and diet. Eating less meat,
		or losing muscle, can raise the number with no real change in filtering. If that may apply to you,
		ask about a <a href="/cystatin-c-gfr-calculator">cystatin C test</a> or a measured GFR.
	</p>

